---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getResponsiveImage } from '../../lib/responsiveImages';

const aboutImage = 'images/about-atelier.png';
const responsiveImage = getResponsiveImage(aboutImage);
---

<BaseLayout
  locale="en"
  title="About"
  description="Why Zone Grise exists and where the project comes from."
  image={aboutImage}
  alternatePath="a-propos"
>
  <section class="about-personal-intro shell">
    <div class="cover-folio">
      <span>About</span>
      <span>Origin of the project</span>
    </div>
    <h1>Why<br /><span>Zone Grise?</span></h1>
  </section>

  {responsiveImage && (
    <figure class="about-visual shell">
      <picture>
        <source
          type="image/webp"
          srcset={responsiveImage.srcset}
          sizes="(max-width: 820px) calc(100vw - 30px), (min-width: 1091px) 960px, 90vw"
        />
        <img
          src={responsiveImage.fallback}
          alt="A creative desk covered with notebooks, paper and notes, including the French phrase ‘Trop d’idées. Pas assez de temps.’ — ‘Too many ideas. Not enough time.’"
          width="1448"
          height="1086"
          loading="eager"
          fetchpriority="high"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </picture>
    </figure>
  )}

  <section class="about-personal shell">
    <aside class="about-personal-aside" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="about-spectrum"></div>
      <span>ZG / origin</span>
    </aside>

    <div class="about-personal-copy">
      <p class="about-opening">I have a lot of ideas in my head.</p>

      <p>Projects, things I want to create, things I would like to learn or build. Often more than I can realistically bring to completion.</p>

      <p>I can easily feel overwhelmed by my own ambitions. I am also fairly perfectionistic: when I make something, I want the result to live up to what I imagine, even when I do not yet have all the skills needed to get there on my own.</p>

      <p>That is the context in which AI started to interest me.</p>

      <p>I see a tool with an enormous range of possibilities. A way to learn, unblock a problem, explore an idea, or simply move forward on projects that would otherwise probably remain somewhere in the back of my mind.</p>

      <p>And I find it difficult not to be fascinated by that.</p>

      <p>But that fascination also comes with a certain discomfort.</p>

      <p>The more I discover these tools, the more I wonder what they change about the way I create. What place I still occupy in what I produce. How far I can rely on them without feeling that I am losing something along the way.</p>

      <p>I feel the same tension when I look at what is happening around AI more broadly.</p>

      <p>Some uses deeply bother me, and part of what I see developing around these tools makes me sad. But I identify just as little with reactions that make the tool itself the source of every problem.</p>

      <p>So I often find myself somewhere between two positions.</p>

      <p class="about-origin">That is where <strong>Zone Grise</strong> came from.</p>

      <p>This blog is where I try to put some order into all these questions, explore this new territory as it takes shape and, I hope, bring a little nuance to places where positions sometimes become very sharp.</p>

      <p>There are still an enormous number of open questions.</p>

      <p class="about-closing">That is probably what makes this gray area so interesting.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
